#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Critical concepts: {#critical-concepts}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"While the measurement of a qubit in a superposition is probabilistic, like "
"flipping a coin, the superposition state itself behaves differently than a "
"flipping coin.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A main difference between a classical probability distribution and a "
"superposition is that the superposition has *phase* coherence, which allows "
"it to interfere constructively or destructively.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The state of a single, isolated qubit can be visualized as a point on the "
"so-called \"Bloch sphere,\" where the relative amplitudes of the "
"$|0\\rangle$ and $|1\\rangle$ components determine the polar angle $\\theta$"
" and the relative phases between the two components determine the azimuthal "
"angle $\\phi$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All single-qubit quantum gates can be viewed as rotations of the vector on "
"this sphere.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "T/F questions: {#tf-questions}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"T/F A quantum superposition is basically the same as a probabilistic event "
"in classical physics, like flipping a coin.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"T/F The length of the Bloch vector describing the state of a single isolated"
" qubit is always 1.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"T/F Single-qubit quantum gates do not change the length of the Bloch "
"vector.\n"
msgstr ""

